{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba skills/mission/prompts/scrutiny-validator.md | sed -n '172,262p' && nl -ba skills/mission/templates/plan.md | sed -n '25,42p'\"","aggregated_output":"   172\t   - Backwards-compatibility code: old code paths kept \"just in case\" (renamed `_` variables, re-exports of removed types, `// removed` comments, deprecated shims). If unused, it should be deleted.\n   173\t   - Fallback lookups: code that tries one path and falls back to another. There should be exactly one lookup path. If it fails, throw an error.\n   174\t   - Belt-and-suspenders: redundant validation that duplicates what the framework, DB, or upstream already guarantees (re-checking auth after middleware, null-checking non-null TypeScript values, re-validating input that a validator already checked).\n   175\t   - Feature flags for dead code: if the old way is being replaced, delete it. Don't gate behind a flag.\n   176\t   - Defensive handling of impossible states: catch blocks or null checks for conditions that cannot occur given current code paths.\n   177\t\n   178\t8. **Code simplify:** Run /simplify against the changed files. \n   179\t\n   180\t9. **Security review:** If the diff touches auth, secrets, user input, network boundaries, or filesystem trust boundaries (copying/moving/promoting files between directories, extracting archives, sandbox-to-production transfers), run /security-review. Otherwise \"N/A.\"\n   181\t\n   182\t10. **Contract assertions:** Mark each assertion PASS, FAIL, or SKIP with a one-line explanation. SKIPs for work deferred to later tasks: append to `mission/deferrals.md` as `task N -- <assertion>`.\n   183\t\n   184\t11. **Behavioral conformance:** For each assertion that describes observable behavior (CLI output, exit codes, file content, API response, shell behavior), write and execute a minimal test script that verifies the behavior by actually running the code. Do not rely solely on reading the code -- run it. Examples:\n   185\t    - CLI flag assertion: run the binary with the flag, check output\n   186\t    - Exit code assertion: run the command, check `$?`\n   187\t    - Shell alias assertion: source the alias, run `type <name>`, check the definition\n   188\t    - API response assertion: curl the endpoint, check the response body\n   189\t    \n   190\t    If an assertion cannot be tested via execution (e.g., internal architecture, code organization, build configuration), mark it as \"verified by inspection\" with an explanation of why execution was not possible.\n   191\t    \n   192\t    If a behavioral test is inconclusive (e.g., the test script errors out, the environment lacks a dependency), mark it as \"verified by inspection (execution inconclusive: <reason>)\" and fall back to code reading. Do not let an inconclusive execution check block the validation.\n   193\t    \n   194\t    Report each assertion's verification method (execution or inspection) in the contract assertions table.\n   195\t\n   196\t12. **Silent skip detection:** Grep test files in the diff for language-specific patterns that cause tests to report PASS while testing nothing. Use the `Detected language` named fact from the deterministic pre-pass to choose the primary language-specific scan, and also inspect file extensions in the diff so mixed-language projects are covered.\n   197\t\n   198\t    Test file detection uses the same language-specific patterns as the file size count exclusions: Rust files in `tests/` or files containing `#[test]`; TypeScript/Node `*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`, or files in `__tests__/`; Python `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, or files in `tests/`.\n   199\t\n   200\t    If the diff contains no test files, report `silent-skip-detection | SKIP | no test files in diff`.\n   201\t\n   202\t    Flag these Rust silent skip patterns:\n   203\t    - Flag `else { return; }` or `else { return }` inside functions annotated with `#[test]` or `#[tokio::test]`.\n   204\t    - Flag `if env::var(\"X\").is_err() { return; }` or similar top-of-test conditional returns based on environment variables.\n   205\t    - Do not flag Rust test functions marked with `#[ignore]`.\n   206\t\n   207\t    Flag these TypeScript/Node silent skip patterns:\n   208\t    - Flag `if (!process.env.X) return` inside `it()`, `test()`, or `describe()` blocks.\n   209\t    - Flag `if (!process.env.X) { return; }` inside `it()`, `test()`, or `describe()` blocks.\n   210\t    - Do not flag `it.skip()`, `describe.skip()`, or `@jest.skip`.\n   211\t\n   212\t    Flag these Python silent skip patterns:\n   213\t    - Flag `if not os.environ.get(\"X\"): return` inside `test_` functions.\n   214\t    - Flag bare `return` at the top of `test_` functions when it is conditional on environment variables.\n   215\t    - Do not flag `@pytest.mark.skip` or `pytest.skip(\"reason\")` with an explicit message.\n   216\t\n   217\t    Any silent skip found is a FAIL because silent skips give false confidence by hiding missing coverage.\n   218\t\n   219\t    Report failures as `silent-skip-detection | FAIL | N silent skips found`. For each instance, list the file path, line number, test function name, and language-appropriate remediation guidance: Rust: \"fail explicitly with `panic!(\"requires X\")` or mark with `#[ignore]`\"; TypeScript: \"throw an error or use `it.skip()`\"; Python: \"call `pytest.skip(\"reason\")` or use `@pytest.mark.skip`\".\n   220\t\n   221\t13. **Diff coverage:** Use the `Detected language` named fact from the deterministic pre-pass to select the coverage tool. Run coverage for the primary language only. In multi-language projects, use the language with the most production source files in the diff; do not run two coverage tools.\n   222\t\n   223\t    First identify changed production source files for the selected language. Use the same production source and standalone test exclusions as the file size count:\n   224\t    - Rust: include `.rs`; exclude files in `tests/`.\n   225\t    - TypeScript/Node: include `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.js`, `.jsx`; exclude `*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`, and files in `__tests__/`.\n   226\t    - Python: include `.py`; exclude `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, and files in `tests/`.\n   227\t\n   228\t    If the diff contains no production source files, report `diff-coverage | SKIP | no production source files in diff`.\n   229\t\n   230\t    Run the language-appropriate coverage tool:\n   231\t    - Rust: run `cargo tarpaulin --out json` first; if that is unavailable or fails because the tool is missing, use `cargo llvm-cov --json` as fallback.\n   232\t    - TypeScript/Node: run `npx jest --coverage --json` or `npx c8 --reporter json`.\n   233\t    - Python: run `pytest --cov --cov-report json`.\n   234\t\n   235\t    If the coverage tool is not installed, first report `diff-coverage | SKIP | <tool> not installed` as a WARNING, then attempt auto-install as a fallback. For Rust, attempt `cargo install cargo-tarpaulin` before retrying tarpaulin. For TypeScript/Node and Python, use the project's existing package manager or environment only when that is clearly configured by the repository; otherwise keep the SKIP result and explain the missing tool. If auto-install succeeds, retry the coverage run. If auto-install fails, keep the SKIP result.\n   236\t\n   237\t    Run `git diff <commit-sha>^ <commit-sha> --name-only` to get changed files. Filter the coverage JSON to those changed files, then count only added lines from `git diff <commit-sha>^ <commit-sha>`. Added lines are lines that appear as additions in the task diff. This is diff coverage: line coverage for new code only, not whole-file or whole-project coverage.\n   238\t\n   239\t    Apply these thresholds to added-line coverage:\n   240\t    - 80% or higher: PASS.\n   241\t    - 70-79%: WARN, include it in the report, and do not fail the task.\n   242\t    - Below 70%: FAIL and trigger the fix loop.\n   243\t\n   244\t    Report success as `diff-coverage | PASS | 85% coverage on changed lines`. Report warnings as `diff-coverage | WARN | 74% coverage on changed lines (target: 80%)`. Report failures as `diff-coverage | FAIL | 55% coverage on changed lines (minimum: 70%)`. Include details listing under-covered files with added-line ranges that lack coverage.\n   245\t\n   246\t14. **Test quality review:** If the diff contains no test files, report `test-quality-review | SKIP | no test files in diff`.\n   247\t\n   248\t    Run /test-quality as a subagent against the full diff, following the same delegation pattern as /review, /simplify, and /security-review. Read the skill markdown findings after the subagent completes. Findings are grouped by severity (P0, P1, P2, P3) and include file:line, evidence, and remediation.\n   249\t\n   250\t    Apply this severity-based result behavior:\n   251\t    - Any P0 finding, including tautological assertions, is a FAIL.\n   252\t    - 3 or more P1 findings are a FAIL. P1 findings include untested critical branches and behavioral changes without tests.\n   253\t    - 1-2 P1 findings are WARNINGS; include them in the report but do not fail the task.\n   254\t    - P2 and P3 findings only are WARNINGS; include them in the report but do not fail the task.\n   255\t    - No findings is PASS.\n   256\t\n   257\t    Report passing output as `test-quality-review | PASS | no findings`. Report warning output as `test-quality-review | WARN | 2 P2, 1 P3 findings`. Report failing output as `test-quality-review | FAIL | 1 P0 finding (tautological assertion in test_list_open)`. Include a summary of findings in the notes column.\n   258\t\n   259\t## Output Format\n   260\t\n   261\tWrite your report to: <absolute path to mission/validations/task-N-scrutiny.md>\n   262\t\n    25\t- Gaps resolved: <added N tasks | deferred N | skipped N>\n    26\t\n    27\t## Engine Config\n    28\tDefaults defined in Phase 6 config.yaml. \n    29\t\n    30\t## Skills to Use\n    31\t- /grill-me -- alignment interview (planning)\n    32\t- /ubiquitous-language -- shared language (planning)\n    33\t- /write-a-prd -- structured PRD from grill conversation (planning)\n    34\t- openspec CLI -- create artifacts sequentially: proposal, specs, design, tasks (planning)\n    35\t- /review -- code review (scrutiny validation)\n    36\t- /simplify -- simplify pass (scrutiny validation)\n    37\t- /security-review -- security check (scrutiny validation, sensitive tasks only)\n    38\t- /tdd -- test-driven development for workers (build phase)\n    39\t- /test-app -- browser tests (user-test validation)\n    40\t- /demo-video -- frontend demo recording (post-validation)\n    41\t- gh pr create --draft (completion, no skill needed)\n    42\t- /opsx:archive -- archive change (post-merge)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
